			<!-- SECTION 3 — FROM KEYNES TO HALL & WOODFORD (Vertical Timeline) -->
			<section class="relative z-10 mb-12">
				<div class="mb-6">
					<div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-amber-400 mb-1">
						<span>⏳</span>
						<span>·</span>
						<span class="lang-zh">宏观思想演化编年史</span>
						<span class="lang-en">Evolutionary Timeline</span>
					</div>
					<h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">
						<span class="lang-zh">从凯恩斯到霍尔与伍德福德 · From Keynes to New Keynesian</span>
						<span class="lang-en">From Keynes to Hall & Woodford · The Timeline</span>
					</h2>
					<p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-400 mt-1">
						<span class="lang-zh">半个世纪以来，宏观稳定政策如何经历破裂、重构并演进为现代央行的标准架构。</span>
						<span class="lang-en">How macroeconomic stabilisation evolved from old hydraulic models through the stagflation crisis into modern micro-founded models.</span>
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="relative border-l border-slate-800 ml-4 sm:ml-6 pl-6 sm:pl-8 space-y-8">
					<!-- Step 1 -->
					<div class="relative">
						<div class="absolute -left-[31px] sm:-left-[39px] top-1.5 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-slate-800 border-2 border-slate-600 flex items-center justify-center">
							<span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-slate-400"></span>
						</div>
						<div class="inline-block px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-xs font-bold text-slate-400 bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 mb-1">
							1950s – 1960s
						</div>
						<h3 class="text-base sm:text-lg font-bold text-white">
							<span class="lang-zh">旧凯恩斯主义教条：宏观水力学杠杆</span>
							<span class="lang-en">Old Keynesian Recipe: Aggregate Hydraulic Levers</span>
						</h3>
						<p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 mt-1 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
							<span class="lang-zh">战后早期宏观经济学将经济视为一台巨大的水力机器。政策制定者认为通胀与失业之间存在稳定的静态替换关系（原始菲利普斯曲线）：萧条时加大刺激，过热时给总需求降温。这种自上而下的调控忽略了微观经济主体的理性预期与自我调整。</span>
							<span class="lang-en">Early postwar macroeconomics treated the economy as a hydraulic aggregate system with an assumed stable trade-off between inflation and unemployment. Policy makers believed manipulating aggregate spending could predictably fine-tune booms and slumps, ignoring individual rational expectations.</span>
						</p>
					</div>

					<!-- Step 2 -->
					<div class="relative">
						<div class="absolute -left-[31px] sm:-left-[39px] top-1.5 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-rose-950 border-2 border-rose-500 flex items-center justify-center">
							<span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-rose-400 animate-pulse"></span>
						</div>
						<div class="inline-block px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-xs font-bold text-rose-400 bg-rose-950/40 border border-rose-800/60 mb-1">
							1970s
						</div>
						<h3 class="text-base sm:text-lg font-bold text-rose-200">
							<span class="lang-zh">滞胀大危机：旧范式彻底瓦解</span>
							<span class="lang-en">1970s Stagflation Crisis: The Old Paradigm Breaks</span>
						</h3>
						<p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 mt-1 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
							<span class="lang-zh">两次石油危机引爆全球滞胀（Stagflation），高通胀与高失业同时爆发，彻底击碎了传统菲利普斯曲线。若工人预期物价上涨并提前要求加薪，单纯刺激需求只会引发通胀螺旋而无法创造就业。“卢卡斯批判（Lucas Critique）”明确指出：忽视微观预期行为的宏观调控注定走向失败。</span>
							<span class="lang-en">The 1970s oil shocks brought stagflation—high inflation and high unemployment simultaneously—shattering the static Phillips curve. Workers and firms anticipated policy actions and demanded higher wages, rendering stimulus ineffective. The Lucas Critique proved that macro models devoid of micro foundations were fatal flaws.</span>
						</p>
					</div>

					<!-- Step 3 -->
					<div class="relative">
						<div class="absolute -left-[31px] sm:-left-[39px] top-1.5 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-teal-950 border-2 border-teal-400 flex items-center justify-center">
							<span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-teal-300"></span>
						</div>
						<div class="inline-block px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-xs font-bold text-teal-300 bg-teal-950/40 border border-teal-800/60 mb-1">
							1978 – 2003
						</div>
						<h3 class="text-base sm:text-lg font-bold text-teal-200">
							<span class="lang-zh">霍尔与伍德福德：在微观地基上重建宏观大厦</span>
							<span class="lang-en">Hall & Woodford: Micro-Foundations Rebuilt</span>
						</h3>
						<p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 mt-1 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
							<span class="lang-zh">霍尔（1978）提出消费随机游走假说，证明前瞻性家庭会在整个生命周期平滑消费；伍德福德（2003 年巨著《利息与价格》）将卡尔沃价格黏性与前瞻性预期严谨结合，构建了新凯恩斯主义理论大厦。他证明中央银行的核心威力不是借贷资金的机械转移，而是锚定全市场对未来利率政策的预期轨迹。</span>
							<span class="lang-en">Hall (1978) proved that forward-looking households smooth consumption over lifetimes based on permanent income. Woodford (2003, *Interest and Prices*) unified sticky-price micro foundations with forward-looking expectations into modern New Keynesian theory, demonstrating the decisive power of communication and the expectations channel.</span>
						</p>
					</div>

					<!-- Step 4 -->
					<div class="relative">
						<div class="absolute -left-[31px] sm:-left-[39px] top-1.5 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-amber-950 border-2 border-amber-400 flex items-center justify-center">
							<span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-amber-300"></span>
						</div>
						<div class="inline-block px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-xs font-bold text-amber-300 bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-800/60 mb-1">
							TODAY
						</div>
						<h3 class="text-base sm:text-lg font-bold text-white">
							<span class="lang-zh">今日全球央行标准：风浪中的动态防御</span>
							<span class="lang-en">Today: Global Standard in Central Banking</span>
						</h3>
						<p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 mt-1 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
							<span class="lang-zh">今天，美联储、欧洲央行、英格兰银行、瑞典央行等全球主要货币当局均将新凯恩斯 DSGE 模型作为日常决策不可或缺的分析基准。从应对 2020 年疫情突发骤降，到应对随后的全球能源供应链瓶颈，前瞻性指引与预期管理机制在无数次实战冲击中守护着现代经济的平稳运行。</span>
							<span class="lang-en">Today, New Keynesian dynamic stochastic general equilibrium (DSGE) models serve as analytical backbones across the Fed, ECB, Bank of England, and Riksbank. Tested by pandemic recessions and post-2022 global supply shocks, forward guidance has proven essential for stabilizing output without unanchoring inflation expectations.</span>
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- SECTION 4 — THE LAUREATES (2 Bio Cards + Prize Facts) -->
			<section class="relative z-10 mb-10">
				<div class="mb-6">
					<div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-teal-400 mb-1">
						<span>🏅</span>
						<span>·</span>
						<span class="lang-zh">诺贝尔奖得主档案</span>
						<span class="lang-en">Laureates Dossier</span>
					</div>
					<h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">
						<span class="lang-zh">2026 诺贝尔经济学奖得主 · The Laureates</span>
						<span class="lang-en">The 2026 Laureates in Economic Sciences</span>
					</h2>
				</div>

				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-6">
					<!-- Robert E. Hall -->
					<div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 hover:border-teal-500/40 transition-colors">
						<div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-3 mb-3">
							<div>
								<div class="text-xs font-mono text-teal-400 font-bold uppercase">STANFORD UNIVERSITY</div>
								<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white">
									<span class="lang-zh">罗伯特·E·霍尔 (Robert E. Hall)</span>
									<span class="lang-en">Robert E. Hall</span>
								</h3>
							</div>
							<span class="text-2xl">🎓</span>
						</div>
						<div class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 mb-3 pb-3 border-b border-slate-800">
							<span class="lang-zh">出生：1943 年 · 美国加利福尼亚州帕洛阿尔托</span>
							<span class="lang-en">Born: 1943 in Palo Alto, California, USA</span>
						</div>
						<p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
							<span class="lang-zh">
								斯坦福大学胡佛研究所高级研究员兼经济学教授。霍尔革新了经济学界对家庭消费与劳动力市场的理解。他提出著名的“消费随机游走假说”，严格证明前瞻性家庭会在整个生命周期平滑开支，即今日的消费取决于对未来持久收入的预期，而非眼前的即时所得。他还深入剖析了衰退期间招聘摩擦与工资黏性对失业波动的长远放大效应，为宏观总需求奠定了坚实的微观行为根基。
							</span>
							<span class="lang-en">
								Robert E. Hall (Stanford University & Hoover Institution) revolutionized how economists model consumption and labor markets. By establishing the Random Walk Hypothesis of consumption, he demonstrated that forward-looking households smooth spending across lifetimes based on expected permanent income rather than transitory windfalls. His pioneering work on labor search frictions and wage rigidity explained why macroeconomic downturns trigger persistent unemployment slumps.
							</span>
						</p>
					</div>

					<!-- Michael Woodford -->
					<div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 hover:border-amber-500/40 transition-colors">
						<div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-3 mb-3">
							<div>
								<div class="text-xs font-mono text-amber-400 font-bold uppercase">COLUMBIA UNIVERSITY</div>
								<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white">
									<span class="lang-zh">迈克尔·伍德福德 (Michael Woodford)</span>
									<span class="lang-en">Michael Woodford</span>
								</h3>
							</div>
							<span class="text-2xl">🏛️</span>
						</div>
						<div class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 mb-3 pb-3 border-b border-slate-800">
							<span class="lang-zh">出生：1955 年 · 美国马萨诸塞州奇科皮</span>
							<span class="lang-en">Born: 1955 in Chicopee, Massachusetts, USA</span>
						</div>
						<p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
							<span class="lang-zh">
								哥伦比亚大学经济学教授。伍德福德构建了现代货币政策的理论基石。在其里程碑著作《利息与价格》（2003）中，他将微观价格黏性与前瞻性理性预期深度结合，构建了标准新凯恩斯主义分析框架。他阐明了货币政策的核心传导渠道在于“预期管理”——央行能够通过公开沟通与可信的前瞻性指引引导市场利率走向，从而在不必承受过度产出损失的情况下平稳达成通胀与就业目标。
							</span>
							<span class="lang-en">
								Michael Woodford (Columbia University) built the theoretical foundations of modern central-bank policy. His definitive treatise *Interest and Prices* (2003) integrated sticky-price micro foundations with forward-looking expectations into the workhorse New Keynesian framework. He proved that monetary policy operates primarily through the expectations channel: credibly signaling future policy trajectories anchors inflation and tames output volatility far more effectively than moving current rates alone.
							</span>
						</p>
					</div>
